## 2. Confirmed bug — `Phaser` used without importing it

Found by grepping the actual source for `Phaser.` usage vs. top-level `import Phaser`.

Most files only use `Phaser.Scene`, `Phaser.GameObjects.Sprite`, etc. as **types** — safe, erased at compile time, no import needed.

But two files use `Phaser.X` as a **runtime value**, with no import at all:

```ts
// src/game/entities/Enemy.ts:95
const dist = Phaser.Math.Distance.Between(...)
```

```ts
// src/game/systems/InventorySystem.ts:28
scene.events.once(Phaser.Scenes.Events.SHUTDOWN, () => {...})
```

TypeScript doesn't flag this because Phaser's `.d.ts` also declares an *ambient global* `namespace Phaser` (legacy script-tag support), so it type-checks fine with zero import. At runtime in an ESM bundle there is no global `Phaser` — only `import Phaser from "phaser"` creates a real binding. These **will** throw once those code paths run (enemy distance checks, inventory scene-shutdown cleanup).

**Fix:** add to the top of both files:
```ts
import Phaser from "phaser";
```

